### Monolith vs Microservices: The Hidden Costs

At a glance this looks like a scaling question, but the real trade-off is organizational, not technical. A monolith keeps all code in one deployable unit, so a developer can grep the whole codebase, run one test suite, and reason about a single transaction boundary. That simplicity breaks down as the team grows past what Conway's Law predicts a single codebase can support - once you have 20+ engineers touching the same repo, deploy queues, merge conflicts, and a shared database schema become the actual bottleneck, not raw performance.

Microservices remove that bottleneck by letting each team own a service end to end, but the "hidden costs" rarely show up in the pitch deck: every synchronous call between services adds a network hop that can fail independently, so you need retries, timeouts, and circuit breakers just to reach the reliability a single in-process function call gave you for free. Testing gets harder too - an integration test for a single business flow now needs several services running together (or a contract-testing setup), and a change that used to be one PR now needs coordinated rollout across teams with backward-compatible API versions.

```typescript
// Monolith: In-process call, fails only if the process is down
async function placeOrder(data: OrderInput) {
  const order = await orderService.create(data);
  await paymentService.charge(order.id); // same process, no network
  return order;
}

// Microservices: needs explicit resiliency for the same logic
async function placeOrder(data: OrderInput) {
  const order = await orderClient.create(data); // network call #1
  await retryWithBackoff(() => paymentClient.charge(order.id), {
    maxRetries: 3,
    timeoutMs: 2000,
  }); // network call #2, can fail independently of call #1
  return order;
}
```

**Common pitfall:** treating the decision as purely technical ("microservices scale better") instead of matching it to team topology. A 4-person team adopting microservices usually ends up with all the coordination overhead of distributed systems and none of the org-scaling benefit, because there's no second team to actually decouple from.

