### Strangler Fig Pattern: Migrating a Legacy Payments Monolith

The pattern puts a routing layer (proxy, feature flag, or API gateway rule) in front of the legacy system and gradually redirects traffic, endpoint by endpoint, to new services - the old system keeps working underneath until every path has been "strangled" and it's safe to decommission. A concrete scenario: migrating a 10-year-old PHP monolith's checkout flow to a new Node.js order service. You don't rewrite checkout in one shot; you first extract read-only endpoints (order history) behind the router, verify parity, then move mutating endpoints (create order) last, because those carry the real risk of data divergence.

The two hardest problems are rarely mentioned in the diagram-level explanation. First, dual writes: during the transition, both systems may need to know about the same order, so you either write to both stores (risking partial failure and drift) or make the legacy system read from the new one via an anti-corruption adapter - the second is safer but slower to build. Second, rollback: once traffic has been routed to the new service for a write path, rolling back means reconciling any writes the new system made that the legacy system never saw, so you need a clear "point of no return" per endpoint, not just a global flag.

```typescript
// Routing layer with per-endpoint cutover state, not a single global switch
const cutoverState: Record<string, 'legacy' | 'new' | 'shadow'> = {
  'GET /orders/:id/history': 'new',    // fully migrated
  'POST /orders': 'shadow',            // new system runs in parallel, legacy still authoritative
  'POST /orders/:id/refund': 'legacy',  // not migrated yet - highest risk path
};

app.use((req, res, next) => {
  const state = cutoverState[routeKey(req)];
  if (state === 'shadow') {
    // fire-and-forget to new system for comparison, legacy response still wins
    newOrderService.create(req.body).catch(logDivergence);
  }
  return state === 'new' ? newOrderService.handle(req, res) : next();
});
```

**Common pitfall:** treating the migration as "done" once the router points at the new system for every route, while ignoring that legacy data (old orders, old customer records) still lives only in the old schema - queries that need historical data now have to fan out to both stores, or you need a one-time backfill before you can actually delete the legacy system.

